  "markdown": "Your room, your wallpaper\n\n# Mr Wallcover Atelier\n\nPhotograph the wall, tell us how wide it is, and see a pattern on it at its real size. The preview is made on your device and never leaves it. It shows scale and layout; a sample in the room shows colour and texture.\n\n## Room preview\n\nThe preview needs JavaScript. Without it, [send us a photo of the wall](https://www.mrwallcover.com/contact/?intent=install) with its width and height and we will advise on the pattern and the quantity, or work the rolls out in the [calculator](https://www.mrwallcover.com/calculator/).\n\n1 The room\n\n- **Your photo**\n\nStand square to the wall in daylight with the whole wall in the frame. The photo stays in this browser tab.\n\nChoose or take a photo\n\n- **Or try our room** ![Before: an empty room at St Michael's Clergy House, with red carpet, white walls and an arched door, before the showroom works.](https://www.mrwallcover.com/media/img/house-of-hackney-st-michaels-03.jpg)\n\nA room photographed before our works, so the wall is bare. Wall size for the demonstration: about 420 × 280 cm.\n\nUse this room\n\nIllustrative preview — not a colour match\n\nFind the wall for me\n\nDrag the four handles to the corners of the wall (arrow keys move a focused handle). “Find the wall” fetches a small recognition model and its runtime, about 15 MB in all, from public code libraries the first time, then runs on your device; check its result, it is a guess.\n\n2 The wall\n\nWidth of the wall, cm\n\ncm\n\nHeight, floor to ceiling or cornice, cm\n\ncm\n\nThe size sets the scale of the pattern. One real measurement is enough: a standard internal door is 198 cm high, a skirting board is often 12 to 15 cm.\n\n3 The pattern\n\nFive ways to bring a pattern in. Each is laid on the wall at the width and repeat you set below.\n\nA link to the wallpaper\n\nRead the link\n\nA link straight to the wallpaper image works where the maker's site allows it. For a product page, save its image and upload or paste it here, then type the roll width and repeat the page states; the link still goes with your enquiry. Pasting the page's text with the sizes into this box fills the fields too.\n\nYour own sample or a saved image\n\nA straight-on photo of a sample, or the maker's image of one repeat. You can also paste an image from anywhere with Ctrl or ⌘ V.\n\nWhat it is\n\n- Wallpaper, repeating\n- Mural, one image fitted to the wall\n\nOr a paint colour\n\nA flat colour on the wall, keeping the room's light. Screen colour is not a paint match.\n\nRoll width, cm\n\ncm\n\nPattern repeat, cm\n\ncm\n\nMatch\n\n- Straight match\n- Half-drop\n- Free match\n\nThe image shows, cm wide\n\ncm\n\nThe image shows, cm high\n\ncm\n\nFollows the roll until you change it. Makers' pictures often show two or three drops side by side, or a square crop: say what the picture really covers.\n\nMove the pattern across\n\nMove the pattern up or down\n\nKeep the room's light and shade\n\nThe illustrative patterns are drawn by us for scale only; they are not products. Use your own sample for the real design. A plain border around a picture is trimmed off. Makers' pictures are rarely a seamless repeat, so joins can show between tiles in the preview that will not show on the wall; the scale is what to judge.\n\n4 Next\n\nDownload the preview\n\n[Rolls for this wall](https://www.mrwallcover.com/calculator/) [Send us this wall](https://www.mrwallcover.com/contact/?intent=install&from=room-preview)\n\nThe calculator takes this wall and roll with you and works out the drops and rolls the way we cut them. The enquiry carries the wall size and pattern as a note; attach the downloaded preview if you like.\n\n## How the preview is made\n\nYour photo is loaded in the browser only; it is not sent to us or to anyone. If you paste a link to a product page and our link reader is switched on, that address alone is sent to it so it can fetch the page. The four corners you place on the wall tell the preview where the wall's rectangle sits in the picture, and the width and height you give turn that rectangle into real centimetres. The pattern is then laid across it drop by drop, at the roll width and repeat you set, with a half-drop staggered on alternate drops, and the light and shade of your own photograph are kept on top. Nothing is uploaded, no account is made and no cookie is set.\n\n### What it can show\n\n- How big a repeat really is on your wall, and how many drops run across it.\n- Where the pattern falls at a corner, a dado or a door case, and what moving it by a few centimetres does.\n- A large-scale design against a small one, in the same room and the same light.\n\n### What it cannot show\n\n- Colour. Screens, phone cameras and printed paper all differ; a sample in the room, in daylight and at night, is the only colour check.\n- Texture and sheen. Grasscloth, silk, vinyl and flock all catch light in ways a flat image does not.\n- The set-out we would choose. Where the first drop goes, how the pattern is centred and how corners are turned are decisions made on site; see our guide to [set-out and pattern matching](https://www.mrwallcover.com/advice/set-out-and-pattern-matching/).\n- Anything in front of the wall. Furniture, radiators and pictures inside the four corners are papered over unless “Find the wall” has cut them out; otherwise crop the photo or move the corners.\n\n### Why we built it this way\n\nRoom visualisers offered by makers apply their own catalogue to a photo; the small print of one widely used visualiser says the scale may vary on your own picture. We are installers: the questions we get are about scale, repeat and where the pattern lands, so this preview asks for one real measurement and is honest about the rest. For a measured room with doors, windows and several walls, [the calculator](https://www.mrwallcover.com/calculator/) gives the quantity and cutting plan, and [a conversation](https://www.mrwallcover.com/contact/?intent=install) gives the set-out.\n\n[Discuss your room](https://www.mrwallcover.com/contact/?intent=install)\n\nBy [Dorin Burcus](https://www.mrwallcover.com/about/#dorin), founder · Last reviewed 9 October 2026\n",
